# Access Roles Shift Planner

Implement a deterministic shift planner for a small on-call team. The team is
organised as a hierarchy of **roles**: permissions are granted to roles and a
role inherits every permission of its parent role (transitively). Each **user**
belongs to exactly one role. A working day has 24 one-hour slots numbered 0 to
23. A **task** occupies a half-open time window `[start_hour, end_hour)`,
requires a permission and a minimum number of simultaneous users, and has an
integer priority. The planner greedily assigns users to tasks under these
constraints.

All code lives in namespace `access_roles_planner`.

## Public API

    #include <map>
    #include <stdexcept>
    #include <string>
    #include <vector>

    namespace access_roles_planner {

    class AccessPlannerError : public std::runtime_error {
    public:
        explicit AccessPlannerError(const std::string& message);
    };

    struct Schedule {
        std::map<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> assignments; // task id -> assigned users
        std::vector<std::string> unscheduled;                        // task ids
    };

    class AccessPlanner {
    public:
        void add_role(const std::string& name, const std::string& parent);
        void grant_permission(const std::string& role, const std::string& permission);
        void add_user(const std::string& name, const std::string& role, int max_hours);
        void set_max_hours(const std::string& user, int max_hours);
        bool has_permission(const std::string& user, const std::string& permission) const;
        std::vector<std::string> users_with_permission(const std::string& permission) const;
        void add_task(const std::string& id, const std::string& permission,
                      int start_hour, int end_hour, int min_staff, int priority);
        Schedule plan() const;
    };

    }

## Errors

Every error is reported by throwing `AccessPlannerError` whose `what()` is
exactly the string shown; `<X>` stands for the offending name inserted
verbatim:

- `"unknown role: <role>"`
- `"role already exists: <name>"`
- `"permission already granted: <permission>"`
- `"user already exists: <name>"`
- `"unknown user: <name>"`
- `"invalid hour limit"`
- `"task already exists: <id>"`
- `"invalid time range"`
- `"invalid staff count"`

## Roles and permissions

`add_role(name, parent)` registers a role. `parent` must be the empty string
(a root role) or the name of a previously registered role; otherwise throw
`"unknown role: <parent>"`. This check comes first, so a role can never be its
own ancestor and cycles are impossible. Then throw `"role already exists:
<name>"` if the name is already taken. Several roles may share the same
parent. Role names, user names, permission strings and task ids are arbitrary
strings compared byte-wise; their content is not otherwise validated.

`grant_permission(role, permission)` grants a permission directly to a role.
Throw `"unknown role: <role>"` if the role is unknown. Throw
`"permission already granted: <permission>"` if that exact role already holds
that exact permission. Granting the same permission to two different roles
(even to a parent and its child) is allowed.

A user's **effective permissions** at any moment are the permissions granted
to the user's own role or to any of its ancestors at that moment. Grants made
after users were added take effect for existing users immediately. Permissions
never flow downward: a parent role does not inherit from its children.

## Users

`add_user(name, role, max_hours)`: checks, in this order — the role exists
(else `"unknown role: <role>"`), the user name is new (else
`"user already exists: <name>"`), and `0 <= max_hours <= 24` (else
`"invalid hour limit"`). `max_hours` is the total number of hours the user may
work across the whole day. A user name may coincide with a role name; the two
namespaces are independent.

`set_max_hours(user, max_hours)`: checks, in this order — the user exists
(else `"unknown user: <user>"`), and the limit is in `0..24` (else
`"invalid hour limit"`).

`has_permission(user, permission)`: throws `"unknown user: <user>"` for an
unknown user; otherwise returns whether the permission is effective for the
user (a permission that was never granted anywhere yields `false`, not an
error).

`users_with_permission(permission)`: returns every user holding the permission
(directly or inherited), in ascending lexicographic order of user name. It
never throws and returns an empty vector when nobody holds it.

## Tasks

`add_task(id, permission, start_hour, end_hour, min_staff, priority)`:
checks, in this order — the id is new (else `"task already exists: <id>"`),
`0 <= start_hour < end_hour <= 24` (else `"invalid time range"`), and
`min_staff >= 1` (else `"invalid staff count"`). `priority` may be any
integer; larger values mean more important. The permission string is not
validated: a task whose permission no user holds simply cannot be staffed.

## plan()

`plan()` is a `const` member function. It builds a schedule from scratch and
does not modify the planner, so it may be called repeatedly with identical
results.

1. Consider the tasks one at a time, ordered by `priority` descending, ties
   broken by `id` ascending (lexicographic).
2. Let `duration = end_hour - start_hour`. Each user starts with
   `remaining_hours = max_hours` and no assigned windows.
3. A user is **eligible** for the current task if:
   - the user's effective permissions include the task's permission;
   - `remaining_hours >= duration`;
   - none of the user's already assigned windows overlaps the task's window.
     Two half-open windows `[a, b)` and `[c, d)` overlap exactly when they
     share at least one hour, i.e. `a < d && c < b`; windows that merely
     touch (`b == c`) do not overlap.
4. Visit the eligible users in ascending lexicographic order of name and take
   the first `min_staff` of them.
5. If at least `min_staff` users were found, the task is scheduled: each
   picked user loses `duration` remaining hours and gains the task's window,
   and `assignments[task id]` becomes the picked user names (already in
   lexicographic order). If fewer than `min_staff` users are eligible, the
   task is not scheduled at all (all-or-nothing): no user's state changes and
   the task's id is appended to `unscheduled`.
6. `unscheduled` lists the unscheduled task ids in the order the tasks were
   considered. `assignments` contains an entry for every scheduled task and
   nothing else.

## Examples

Example 1 — permissions:

    AccessPlanner planner;
    planner.add_role("staff", "");
    planner.add_role("senior", "staff");
    planner.grant_permission("staff", "ticket");
    planner.grant_permission("senior", "deploy");
    planner.add_user("ada", "senior", 8);
    planner.add_user("ben", "staff", 4);

    planner.has_permission("ada", "ticket")  // true, inherited from "staff"
    planner.has_permission("ben", "deploy")  // false, no downward inheritance

Example 2 — scheduling:

    AccessPlanner planner;
    planner.add_role("ops", "");
    planner.grant_permission("ops", "monitor");
    planner.add_user("zoe", "ops", 4);
    planner.add_user("amy", "ops", 4);
    planner.add_task("t1", "monitor", 9, 12, 1, 10);
    planner.add_task("t2", "monitor", 9, 12, 1, 5);
    planner.add_task("t3", "monitor", 10, 11, 1, 1);
    Schedule s = planner.plan();

`t1` (priority 10) is considered first and gets `amy`, the lexicographically
smaller eligible user. `t2` gets `zoe`. `t3` finds both users already busy
(both hold the 9–12 window), so:

    s.assignments.at("t1") == {"amy"}
    s.assignments.at("t2") == {"zoe"}
    s.unscheduled == {"t3"}

