};

class AddressNormalizer {
public:
    AddressNormalizer();
    void set_default_country(const std::string& country_code);
    const std::string& default_country() const;
    static bool is_supported_country(const std::string& country_code);
    NormalizedAddress normalize(const std::string& street_line,
                                const std::string& house_number,
                                const std::string& postal_code,
                                const std::string& city,
                                const std::string& country_code = "") const;
};
```

* The constructor sets the default country to `"DE"`.
* `default_country()` returns the current default country.
* `to_line()` returns the one-line form:
  `street + " " + house_number + ", " + postal_code + " " + city + ", " + country_code`.

## General rule: whitespace normalization

Every input string is first **collapsed**: leading and trailing whitespace is
removed and every internal run of whitespace becomes a single space. All
validation, normalization and error messages below operate on the collapsed
form. An input that collapses to the empty string is called *blank*.

## Step 1 — Country resolution

The supported countries are exactly: `AT`, `CH`, `DE`, `FR`, `NL`, `US`.

For `normalize`:
* If the collapsed `country_code` is blank, the default country is used.
* Otherwise it must consist of exactly two ASCII letters (either case).
  Anything else throws `AddressError` with
  `"invalid country code: <collapsed input>"` (the input's case is preserved).
* The code is upper-cased; if the upper-case form is not supported, throw
  `AddressError` with `"unsupported country code: <upper-case code>"`.
* The resolved country code (always upper-case) is stored in the result.

`set_default_country` validates exactly the same way, except that a blank
argument throws `AddressError("missing country code")`. On success it stores
the upper-case code as the new default.

`is_supported_country` collapses its argument and returns `true` if and only if
it consists of two letters whose upper-case form is supported. It never throws.

## Step 2 — Street

* Blank street line: throw `AddressError("missing street")`.
* If the collapsed street contains any ASCII digit, throw
  `AddressError("street must not contain digits: <collapsed street>")`.
* If the street ends with `'.'`, the period is removed.
* If the lower-cased **final** token is a known street-type abbreviation, it is
  replaced by the full form. Only the final token is ever expanded:

  | abbreviation | full form | abbreviation | full form |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `st` | `street` | `ln` | `lane` |
  | `rd` | `road` | `pl` | `place` |
  | `ave` | `avenue` | `sq` | `square` |
  | `blvd` | `boulevard` | `ter` | `terrace` |
  | `dr` | `drive` | `ct` | `court` |

* Finally the street is **title-cased**: each word is first lower-cased, then
  its first character is upper-cased — except for connector words, which stay
  entirely lower-case when they are not the first word. The connector words
  are: `am, an, auf, and, de, den, der, im, in, of, the, und, van, von, zum,
  zur`.

## Step 3 — House number

* Blank input: throw `AddressError("missing house number")`.
* Otherwise the collapsed input must match: **one to four ASCII digits**,
  optionally followed by **one space** and **one or two ASCII letters**, and
  nothing else. Any violation throws
  `AddressError("invalid house number: <collapsed input>")`.
* The canonical form drops the optional space and upper-cases the letters:
  `"12 b"` → `"12B"`, `"12b"` → `"12B"`, `"1234"` → `"1234"`.

## Step 4 — Postal code

A blank postal code throws `AddressError("missing postal code")`. Any other
violation throws
`AddressError("invalid postal code for <CC>: <collapsed input>")`, where `<CC>`
is the resolved upper-case country code.

* `DE`, `FR`: exactly five digits.
* `AT`, `CH`: exactly four digits.
* `NL`: four digits followed either immediately by two letters, or by one
  space and two letters. Canonical form: `"1234 AB"` (letters upper-cased).
* `US`: five digits, or five digits + `'-'` + four digits (the dash is kept).

## Step 5 — City

A blank city throws `AddressError("missing city")`. Otherwise the city is
title-cased with exactly the same rule as the street (connector words stay
lower-case unless they are the first word).

## Validation order

`normalize` performs the steps strictly in the order above: country, street,
house number, postal code, city. Only the first failing rule throws.

