# TinyConf Linter and Migrator

## The TinyConf v1 format

A TinyConf v1 document is text whose lines are separated by `\n`. Lines are numbered from 1 (blank lines and comments count). Every line is one of:

* **blank** — empty or containing only spaces, tabs and/or a trailing `\r`;
* **comment** — the first non-whitespace character is `#`; the whole line is ignored;
* **section header** — `[name]`, possibly surrounded by whitespace;
* **entry** — `key = value`, split at the *first* `=` character. Both sides are trimmed of leading/trailing spaces, tabs and `\r`. An inline `#` is part of the value (it is *not* a comment).

The implicit section before the first section header is the **top level**; its name is the empty string. Section names and keys are case-sensitive. A key name is *valid* only if it matches `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`.

**Schema.** The known sections and their known keys are:

| section | known keys |
|---|---|
| top level (`""`) | `app_name`, `title`, `version` |
| `server` | `host`, `port`, `debug` |
| `logging` | `level`, `verbose` |

Any other section name or key is *unknown*.

## Public API (namespace `config_linter_migrator`)

```cpp
struct Issue { int line; std::string code; std::string message; };   // line is 1-based, 0 = not tied to a line
struct Entry { std::string key; std::string value; };
struct Section { std::string name; std::vector<Entry> entries; };
struct Document { std::vector<Section> sections; };

struct LintConfig {
    bool check_duplicates = true;   // rules E002, E006
    bool check_key_names = true;    // rule E003
    bool check_schema = true;       // rules E004, E005
    bool warn_empty_values = true;  // rule W001
    bool warn_deprecated = true;    // rule W002
};

enum class MigrationStatus { Ok, CompletedWithWarnings, Failed };

struct MigrationResult {
    MigrationStatus status;
    std::string migrated;           // empty when status == Failed
    std::vector<Issue> errors;
    std::vector<Issue> warnings;
};

struct ParseError : std::runtime_error {
    int line;
    ParseError(int line_number, const std::string& message);
};

Document parse(const std::string& text);
std::vector<Issue> lint(const std::string& text);
std::vector<Issue> lint(const std::string& text, const LintConfig& config);
MigrationResult migrate(const std::string& text);
```

### `parse` (strict)

Parses the document and returns it. It throws `ParseError` (with `what() == "syntax error"` and the 1-based `line`) on the **first** line that is a syntax error. A syntax error is:

* a section-header line that does not end with `]` after trimming (e.g. `[server`), or whose bracket content trims to empty (e.g. `[]` or `[ ]`);
* a non-blank, non-comment line that contains no `=` at all.

Everything else parses, including duplicate headers, duplicate keys, unknown names and invalid key names (those are lint concerns, not syntax). The returned `Document` contains, in order of appearance: one `Section` per section header (a repeated header yields a second, separate `Section`), plus the implicit top-level `Section` **only if** at least one entry appears before the first header. Comments and blank lines never appear.

### `lint` (tolerant, configurable)

`lint` never throws. A syntax error becomes an `E001` issue and scanning continues with the next line. Issues are returned in line order; within a single line they appear in the order listed below (for section headers: E006 before E004; for entries: E002, E005, W001, W002).

| code | message (exact) | rule |
|---|---|---|
| `E001` | `syntax error` | line is not valid per the grammar |
| `E006` | `duplicate section 'NAME'` | a section header whose name was already seen |
| `E004` | `unknown section 'NAME'` | section name not in the schema (each occurrence is reported) |
| `E003` | `invalid key name 'KEY'` | key does not match `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*` (including an empty key). When E003 fires, no other rule applies to that line |
| `E002` | `duplicate key 'KEY'` | key already seen in the same section |
| `E005` | `unknown key 'KEY' in section 'SECTION'` | key not in the schema of a known section. For the top level, `SECTION` is rendered as `(top-level)`. Keys inside unknown sections are *not* schema-checked |
| `W001` | `empty value for key 'KEY'` | trimmed value is empty |
| `W002` | `deprecated key 'title'` | top-level entry `title` |

`lint(text)` uses the default `LintConfig` (all checks enabled). The config flags switch off the rule groups shown in the struct.

### `migrate` (v1 → v2)

1. Lint with all checks enabled. The `E*` issues go to `errors`, the `W*` issues to `warnings`.
2. Apply the v2 semantic checks below (only to known keys in known sections), appending to `errors` in line order of the triggering entry; `M004`, if it occurs, is always appended last:

| code | message (exact) | rule |
|---|---|---|
| `M001` | `conflicting keys: 'title' and 'app_name' both present` | both top-level keys occur; reported at the `title` line |
| `M002` | `invalid port 'VALUE'` | `server.port` value is not digits-only in the range 1–65535 |
| `M005` | `invalid boolean value 'VALUE' for key 'KEY'` | `server.debug` / `logging.verbose` value is not (case-insensitively) `yes`, `no`, `on`, `off`, `true` or `false` |
| `M003` | `invalid log level 'VALUE'` | `logging.level` value is not (case-insensitively) `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error` |
| `M004` | `missing version` | no top-level `version` entry with a non-empty value; line is the `version` line if the entry exists, otherwise 0 |

3. If `errors` is non-empty: `status == Failed`, `migrated` is the empty string (warnings are kept) and the result is returned.
4. Otherwise build the v2 text:
   * top-level `title` is renamed to `app_name` in place;
   * booleans are normalized to `true` / `false` (lowercase the input, then map `yes`/`on`/`true` → `true`, `no`/`off`/`false` → `false`);
   * `logging.level` is lowercased; every other value is copied verbatim after trimming (numbers are not reformatted);
   * the relative order of entries inside each section is preserved;
   * layout: top-level entries first (no header), then `[server]` (only if it has entries), then `[logging]` (only if it has entries) — regardless of their order in the input;
   * an entry renders as `key = value`; an entry with an empty value renders as `key =` (no trailing space); lines are joined with a single `\n` and there is no trailing newline.
5. `status` is `Ok` when `warnings` is empty, otherwise `CompletedWithWarnings`.

## Examples

Lint:

```
title = demo
colour = blue
