## The cause

The bug is a strict-vs-non-strict comparison in the `FindSlot` sweep loop:

```go
if c+duration < iv[0] {
    break
}
```

The candidate meeting is the half-open interval `[c, c+duration)`, and it overlaps `[iv[0], iv[1])` only if `iv[0] < c+duration`. When `c+duration == iv[0]`, the meeting merely **touches** the start of the busy interval, which — per the spec ("touching end-to-end is not an overlap") — is perfectly valid. But because the break condition uses `<` instead of `<=`, the loop fails to stop in the equality case, then falls into `if c < iv[1]` (which is true, since `iv[0] == c+duration > c`), and wrongly jumps the candidate to `iv[1]`, skipping the valid slot.

In your example: after the first block `[0,100)` pushes the candidate to `c = 100`, the second block `[160,300)` is checked. `c+duration` is exactly `160 == iv[0]`, so the loop should break and return `100, true`. Instead it advances the candidate past the second block. (With the merged intervals `[[0,100),[160,300)]` the code as written actually lands on `300`, not `160` — but either way it misses the valid slot at `100`; the root cause is the same off-by-one.)

The fix is one character: break when `c+duration <= iv[0]`.

I checked the rest of the file against the spec (validation order, deep copy of `SkipWeeks`, union computation, unclamped widening, `duration == 1440` edge where `limit == dayStart`, and intervals from neighboring days that lie entirely before the candidate — those are skipped correctly by the `c < iv[1]` guard). Merging *touching* intervals in `mergeIntervals` is also safe for `FindSlot`: if two widened intervals touch, any candidate ending exactly at the touch point would overlap the earlier interval anyway, so no valid slot is lost.

