    public WarehousePathsPlanner(int width, int height)
    public WarehousePathsPlanner withShelf(int x, int y)
    public WarehousePathsPlanner withOneWayAisle(int x, int y, char direction)
    public WarehousePathsPlanner withDockingBay(int x, int y)
    public WarehousePathsPlanner withNarrowAisle(int x, int y)
    public Route planRoute(char robotType, Position start, java.util.List<Position> stops)

    public record Position(int x, int y) { }
    public record Route(java.util.List<Position> cells, int cost) { }
    public static final class LayoutException extends RuntimeException {
        public LayoutException(String message) { super(message); }
    }
    public static final class RoutePlanningException extends RuntimeException {
        public RoutePlanningException(String message) { super(message); }
    }
}
```

The four `with...` methods install the feature and return the planner itself so
that calls can be chained. A planner may be used for several `planRoute` calls.

## Examples

**Example 1 — detour around shelves.** A 5x4 grid with shelves at `(1,0)`,
`(1,1)` and `(1,2)` (leaving a gap in the bottom row). A small robot routes from
`(0,0)` to `(2,0)`:

```
cells: (0,0) (0,1) (0,2) (0,3) (1,3) (2,3) (2,2) (2,1) (2,0)
cost:  10        (8 moves + 2 turn penalties)
```

**Example 2 — one-way aisle.** A 3x3 grid with a one-way aisle at `(1,1)`
pointing `'E'`. A small robot at `(1,1)` must reach `(1,2)`. It may not leave
`(1,1)` going south, so it moves E, S, W:

```
cells: (1,1) (2,1) (2,2) (1,2)
cost:  5        (1 + 2 + 2: two turn penalties)
```

