Complete the implementation below so that it satisfies the specification.

Specification:

# Citation Styles Index

Implement a small library crate that maintains a collection of bibliography
entries and renders citations in a fixed **author–date** style (a simplified
relative of APA). The crate is a specialised data structure: a
`CitationIndex` that stores validated entries and derives all formatting
from them.

## Data model

```rust
pub struct Entry {
    pub key: String,          // unique identifier, e.g. "knuth1984"
    pub authors: Vec<String>, // non-empty, each "Surname, Given"
    pub year: u16,
    pub title: String,
}
```

Entries only ever enter the index through `add`, which validates them. Every
entry stored in the index therefore satisfies all validation rules below.
The index preserves insertion order, which is observable through
disambiguation suffixes.

### The lead surname

For any stored entry, the **lead surname** is the part of the *first* author
string before its first comma (e.g. `"Knuth, D. E."` → `"Knuth"`).

## Validation rules (applied by `add`, in this exact order)

1. **Key**: must be non-empty and contain no character for which
   `char::is_whitespace` is true. Otherwise `Err(CitationError::InvalidKey(key))`.
2. **Authors list**: must be non-empty. Otherwise `Err(CitationError::NoAuthors)`.
3. **Each author**, checked in order: an author string is valid if
   - it is non-empty,
   - it has no leading or trailing whitespace (`author.trim() == author`),
   - it contains **exactly one** `','`,
   - the part before the comma (the surname) is non-empty and has no
     leading or trailing whitespace of its own,
   - the part after the comma is non-empty after trimming.

   On the first invalid author: `Err(CitationError::InvalidAuthor(author))`
   with the offending string as payload.
   (`"Knuth, D. E."`, `"Knuth,D.E."` are valid; `"Knuth"`, `"Knuth,"`,
   `" Knuth, D."`, `"Knuth , D."`, `"One, A., Two, B."` are not.)
4. **Year**: must be in `1000..=9999` inclusive. Otherwise
   `Err(CitationError::InvalidYear(year))`.
5. **Title**: must be non-empty after trimming (it is stored unchanged).
   Otherwise `Err(CitationError::EmptyTitle)`.
6. **Duplicate key**: if the key already exists in the index:
   `Err(CitationError::DuplicateKey(key))`.
7. **Disambiguation capacity**: at most 26 entries may share the same lead
   surname *and* year. If adding this entry would create a 27th member of
   such a group: `Err(CitationError::DisambiguationOverflow)`.

On success the entry is appended (insertion order) and `Ok(())` is returned.

## Disambiguation suffixes

Define the **group** of an entry as all stored entries with the same lead
surname and the same year, listed in insertion order. If the group has only
one member, the entry has no suffix. Otherwise the entry at position `i`
(zero-based) in the group gets the suffix `char` `('a' as u8 + i) as char`
(`"a"`, `"b"`, …). Suffixes are recomputed on demand, so removing an entry
shifts the suffixes of the remaining members. Note that the group is keyed
by *lead surname only*: two different authors who share a surname and a year
(say `"Knuth, D. E."` and `"Knuth, J. A."`, both 1984) do collide and both
get suffixes.

## Public API

```rust
impl CitationIndex {
    pub fn new() -> Self;
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize;
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool;
    pub fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<&Entry>;
    pub fn add(&mut self, entry: Entry) -> Result<(), CitationError>;
    pub fn remove(&mut self, key: &str) -> Result<Entry, CitationError>;
    pub fn in_text(&self, key: &str) -> Result<String, CitationError>;
    pub fn in_text_with_page(&self, key: &str, page: &str) -> Result<String, CitationError>;
    pub fn cite_many(&self, keys: &[&str]) -> Result<String, CitationError>;
    pub fn bibliography(&self) -> String;
}
```

`CitationIndex` should implement `Default` (equivalent to `new`).

`remove` returns the removed entry, or `Err(CitationError::UnknownKey(key))`
if absent.

## In-text citations

The **author part** for an entry is:

- 1 author: the lead surname, e.g. `Knuth`
- 2 authors: `S1 & S2` (both lead surnames), e.g. `Knuth & Lamport`
- 3 or more authors: `S1 et al.` e.g. `Knuth et al.`

The **core** is `"{author part}, {year}{suffix}"`.

- `in_text(key)` returns `"({core})"`, e.g. `"(Knuth, 1984)"`,
  `"(Knuth, 1984a)"`. Unknown key → `Err(CitationError::UnknownKey(key))`.
- `in_text_with_page(key, page)` first validates the page: it must be
  non-empty and consist only of ASCII digits; otherwise
  `Err(CitationError::InvalidPage(page))` — this check happens *before* the
  key lookup. On success it returns `"({core}, p. {page})"`, e.g.
  `"(Knuth, 1984, p. 42)"`. Leading zeros are kept verbatim (`"007"` →
  `p. 007`).

### `cite_many`

- An empty slice → `Err(CitationError::NoCitations)`.
- Then the keys are processed in order. For each key: if it was already seen
  in this call → `Err(CitationError::DuplicateCitation(key))`; if it is not
  in the index → `Err(CitationError::UnknownKey(key))`.
- Otherwise the cores of the selected entries are sorted ascending by
  `(lead surname, year, suffix)` using standard `str` ordering for strings,
  joined with `"; "`, and wrapped in one pair of parentheses:
  `"(Knuth, 1984; Knuth, 1986; Lamport, 1994)"`.

## Bibliography

`bibliography()` returns all entries as lines joined with `"\n"` (empty
index → empty string), sorted by `(lead surname, year, suffix)` ascending,
standard `str` ordering. Each line is:

`{author list} ({year}{suffix}). {title}{terminator}`

where the **author list** is:

- 1 author: the author string as stored, e.g. `Knuth, D. E.`
- 2 or more: all but the last joined with `", "`, then `", & "` and the
  last, e.g. `Knuth, D. E., & Lamport, L.` or
  `Knuth, D. E., Lamport, L., & Bibby, D.`

and the **terminator** is `"."` unless the title already ends with `"."`,
`"!"` or `"?"`, in which case nothing is appended.

## Example

```rust
let mut index = CitationIndex::new();
index.add(Entry {
    key: "knuth1984".to_string(),
    authors: vec!["Knuth, D. E.".to_string()],
    year: 1984,
    title: "The TeXbook".to_string(),
}).unwrap();
index.add(Entry {
    key: "knuth1984b".to_string(),
    authors: vec!["Knuth, D. E.".to_string()],
    year: 1984,
    title: "TeX and Metafont".to_string(),
}).unwrap();

assert_eq!(index.in_text("knuth1984").unwrap(), "(Knuth, 1984a)");
assert_eq!(index.in_text_with_page("knuth1984b", "33").unwrap(),
           "(Knuth, 1984b, p. 33)");
assert_eq!(index.cite_many(&["knuth1984b", "knuth1984"]).unwrap(),
           "(Knuth, 1984a; Knuth, 1984b)");
assert_eq!(index.bibliography(),
           "Knuth, D. E. (1984a). The TeXbook.\nKnuth, D. E. (1984b). TeX and Metafont.");
```

Everything must be deterministic: no randomness, time, I/O, threads, or
environment access. Use only the standard library.

